---
/** "How do I get my GPX file?": export steps for the common apps. Used on the Post your round page. */
---
<details class="gpx-help">
  <summary>How do I get my GPX file?</summary>
  <div class="gpx-help-body">
    <p>
      We need the file itself: a link to your activity won’t work. Most apps only export from their website, so on a
      phone open the site in your browser, not the app.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Strava:</strong> open your activity on <a href="https://www.strava.com" rel="noopener">strava.com</a>,
        open the <b>⋯</b> menu and choose <em>Export GPX</em>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Garmin Connect:</strong> open the activity on{' '}<a href="https://connect.garmin.com" rel="noopener">connect.garmin.com</a>, click the cog at the top right and choose <em>Export to GPX</em>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Komoot:</strong> open the tour you recorded on <a href="https://www.komoot.com" rel="noopener">komoot.com</a>,
        open the <b>⋯</b> menu and download the GPX file.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Apple Watch, or an app that can’t export:</strong> let it sync to Strava, then export from Strava as above.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The file lands in <em>Downloads</em> (on an iPhone: Files › Downloads). Then tap the postbox and choose it.
      Still stuck? <a href="/contact/">Get in touch</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</details>

<style>
  .gpx-help { margin-top: 0.8rem; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px; background: var(--paper-2); }
  .gpx-help summary { cursor: pointer; padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--fell); }
  .gpx-help-body { padding: 0 0.9rem 0.8rem; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .gpx-help-body ul { margin: 0 0 0.7rem; padding-left: 1.1rem; display: grid; gap: 0.4rem; }
  .gpx-help-body p { margin: 0 0 0.7rem; }
  .gpx-help-body p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
</style>
